{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nematicides (+ \"removed from the market\" withdrawn banned phased-out) (+ health environmental toxicity))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#5_3210664013","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1479","rank":1,"snippet":"Vegetables (including Herbs) Plant Protection Products Occupational exposure to chemicals and reproductive health Male effects Aldicarb Uses Lawn and Turf 47.2.2.3 Nematodes and Nematicides Recommended publications \" , \"segment\": \"In different countries, different ranges of fumigants (e.g. methyl bromide and dichloropropene), precursors of fumigants (e.g. dazomet) and nonvolatile nematicides (in granular form such as aldicarb) are still authorized for use but the list is becoming more and more restricted because of the negative <cover>environmental impact of the chemicals. Methylbromide, for example, which has a remarkably wide-spectrum activity (including weeds, soil fungi, nematodes and insects), will be banned in all countries of the European Union in 2005. Nematicides </cover>protect young plantlets during the first stages of their development and allow their satisfactory installation and subsequent productivity. None the less, none of the nematicides can eradicate nematodes from the soil because individuals can usually survive in deeper levels where they can escape chemical diffusion; reinfestation of the plants will inevitably occur after a few months or years. It is absolutely necessary that preplanting chemical treatments, which act only against nematode stages surviving in the bare soil, are combined with the use of "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"2/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#5_3210664013","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly states that authorized fumigants and nonvolatile nematicides are becoming increasingly restricted because of negative environmental impact, and gives methyl bromide’s EU ban as an example.","learned":"Nematicide availability has declined because environmental impacts led to tighter authorization lists and bans, including methyl bromide in the EU."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479","reason":"This query found direct evidence about nematicide market restriction due to environmental impacts; continue for additional non-duplicate supporting documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#4_3210661262","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1479","rank":2,"snippet":"Soil Nematicides Applying soil nematicides is justified when severe attacks are observed (high nematode densities in the previous crop) and in cases of replant sickness symptoms following repeated rose crops in the same field or greenhouse. In different countries, different ranges of fumigants (e.g. methyl bromide and dichloropropene), precursors of fumigants (e.g. dazomet) and nonvolatile nematicides (in granular form such as aldicarb) are still authorized for use but the list is becoming more and more restricted because of the negative <cover>environmental impact of the chemicals. Methylbromide, for example, which has a remarkably wide-spectrum activity (including weeds, soil fungi, nematodes and insects), will be banned in all countries of the European Union in 2005. Nematicides </cover>protect young plantlets during the first stages of their development and allow their satisfactory installation and subsequent productivity. None the less, none of the nematicides can eradicate nematodes from the soil because individuals can usually survive in deeper levels where they can escape chemical diffusion; reinfestation of the plants will inevitably occur after a few months or years.\" , \"start_char\": 3053.000000 , \"title\": \"Nematicide - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\" , \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"3/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#4_3210661262","relevance":3,"reason":"This ScienceDirect overview passage directly answers why nematicide options have been lost: authorized products are increasingly restricted because of negative environmental impacts, with methyl bromide’s EU ban as an example.","learned":"Environmental impact is a direct cited reason for shrinking nematicide authorization lists; methyl bromide was banned in the EU by 2005."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479","reason":"The current query is yielding direct evidence on bans/withdrawals and environmental causes; continue to harvest additional relevant documents if available."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_1667865910#16_2916726411","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1479","rank":3,"snippet":"treatment zone of soil fumigants. This allows many Nanidorus minor to escape being killed by fumigant treatments. Population numbers of this nematode are known to rebound following soil fumigation to numbers higher than if no treatment were used. Therefore, soil fumigants, while effective treatments for other plant-parasitic nematodes in Florida, often are not recommended for management of Nanidorus minor. Continuous cultivation of highly susceptible crops such as corn or sorghum can build up populations of P. minor to damaging numbers and may require the use of nematicides on subsequent crops. Summer legumes such as velvetbean or cowpea tend to keep populations of P. minor low and may reduce reliance on nematicides. In the past, organophosphate and carbamate nematicides such as aldicarb and fenamiphos were very effective for management of Nanidorus minor. However, these <cover>nematicides have been withdrawn due to environmental </cover>and health concerns.\" , \"start_char\": 6216.000000 , \"title\": \"EENY339/IN616: Stubby-Root Nematode, Nanidorus minor (Colbran) Siddiqi (syn. Paratrichodorus minor, P. christiei, Trichodorus minor, T. christiei) (Nematoda: Adenophorea: Triplonchida: Diphtherophorina: Trichodoridea: Trichodoridae)\" , \"url\": \"http://edis.ifas.ufl.edu/in616\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"4/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_1667865910#16_2916726411","relevance":3,"reason":"The extension document directly states that previously effective organophosphate and carbamate nematicides such as aldicarb and fenamiphos have been withdrawn due to environmental and health concerns.","learned":"Aldicarb and fenamiphos are examples of lost nematicides; withdrawals were due to environmental and health concerns."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479","reason":"The query continues to produce highly relevant evidence on specific nematicides withdrawn because of health and environmental concerns."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1479"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_1667865910#17_2916728807","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1479","rank":4,"snippet":"Therefore, soil fumigants, while effective treatments for other plant-parasitic nematodes in Florida, often are not recommended for management of Nanidorus minor. Continuous cultivation of highly susceptible crops such as corn or sorghum can build up populations of P. minor to damaging numbers and may require the use of nematicides on subsequent crops. Summer legumes such as velvetbean or cowpea tend to keep populations of P. minor low and may reduce reliance on nematicides. In the past, organophosphate and carbamate nematicides such as aldicarb and fenamiphos were very effective for management of Nanidorus minor. However, these <cover>nematicides have been withdrawn due to environmental </cover>and health concerns. For current nematicide recommendations go to the UF EDIS webpage at https://edis.ifas.ufl.edu/. Selected References Christie JR, Perry VG. 1951. A root disease of plants caused by a nematode of the genus Trichodorus. Science 113:491-493. Decreamer W. 1991.\" , \"start_char\": 6918.000000 , \"title\": \"EENY339/IN616: Stubby-Root Nematode, Nanidorus minor (Colbran) Siddiqi (syn. Paratrichodorus minor, P. christiei, Trichodorus minor, T. christiei) (Nematoda: Adenophorea: Triplonchida: Diphtherophorina: Trichodoridea: Trichodoridae)\" , \"url\": \"http://edis.ifas."}}
