{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ nematicide nematicides) (+ aldicarb fenamiphos oxamyl carbofuran) (+ withdrawn cancelled banned) (+ environmental health concerns))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_614024996#16_563345009","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1480","rank":6,"snippet":"Hosts (Back to Top) Management (Back to Top) Selected References (Back to Top) \" , \"segment\": \"Therefore,  soil fumigants, while effective treatments for other plant-parasitic nematodes in Florida, often are  not recommended for management of Nanidorus minor . Continuous cultivation of highly susceptible crops such as corn or sorghum can build up  populations of Nanidorus minor to damaging numbers and may require the use of nematicides on  subsequent crops. Summer legumes such as velvetbean or cowpea tend to keep populations of Nanidorus minor low and may reduce reliance on nematicides. In the past, organophosphate and carbamate nematicides such as aldicarb and <cover>fenamiphos were very effective for management of Nanidorus minor. However, these nematicides have been withdrawn due to environmental </cover>and health concerns. For current nematicide recommendations go to the UF EDIS webpage. Selected References (Back to Top) Christie JR, Perry VG. 1951. A root disease of plants caused by a nematode of the genus Trichodorus. Science 113:\" , \"start_char\": 7142.000000 , \"title\": \"stubby-root nematode\" , \"url\": \"http://entnemdept.ufl.edu/creatures/nematode/stubbyroot/paratrichodorus_minor.htm\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#4_3210661262","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#5_3210664013","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_1667865910#16_2916726411","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_1667865910#17_2916728807","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_614024996#15_563342684","rank":5,"relevance":3}]}}
