{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ dbcp edb \"methyl bromide\" aldicarb fenamiphos) (+ nematicide nematicides fumigant) (+ banned withdrawn phased-out discontinued) (+ health environmental ozone groundwater))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_2557014800#9_2827975156","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1502","rank":2,"snippet":"v2.1_doc_16_2557014800#9_2827975156\" , \"end_char\": 7920.000000 , \"headings\": \"Bromomethane Bromomethane Bromomethane Contents Occurrence and manufacture Uses Regulation Alternatives Australia New Zealand United States Chile Health effects Excessive exposure See also References External links \" , \"segment\": \"A list of alternative fumigants is available for goods imported from Europe (in what's known as the BICON database), where methyl bromide fumigation has been <cover>banned. Alternatively, the department allows containers from Europe to be fumigated with methyl bromide on arrival to Australia. New Zealand In New Zealand, bromomethane is used as a fumigant for whole logs destined for export. Environmental </cover>groups and the Green Party oppose its use. In May 2011 the Environmental Risk Management Authority (ERMA) introduced new rules for its use which restrict the level of public exposure to the fumigant, set minimum buffer zones around fumigation sites, provide for notification to nearby residents and require users to monitor air quality during fumigations and report back to ERMA each year. All methyl bromide fumigations must use recapture technology by 2021. United States In the United States bromomethane is regulated as ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_1062712741#2_1295395898","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
