{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"synthetic nematicides\" \"chemical nematicides\" nematicides) (+ banned withdrawn restricted) (+ toxic toxicity \"environmental impact\" \"human health\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_3182786751#0_3669820684","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1505","rank":4,"snippet":"Find sources: \" Toxicity class\" – news · newspapers · books · scholar · JSTOR (May 2016) ( Learn how and when to remove this template message) Indian toxicity label system Toxicity symbol for European Toxicity Class I and II Toxicity class refers to a classification system for pesticides that has been created by a national or international government-related or -sponsored organization. It addresses the acute toxicity of agents such as soil fumigants, fungicides, herbicides, insecticides, miticides, molluscicides, <cover>nematicides, or rodenticides . Contents 1 General considerations 2 By jurisdiction 2.1 World Health Organization 2.2 European Union 2.3 India 2.4 United States 2.4.1 Toxicity Class I 2.4.2 Toxicity Class II 2.4.3 Toxicity Class III 2.4.4 Toxicity Class IV 2.4.5 General versus restricted </cover>use 3 See also 4 References General considerations Assignment to a toxicity class is based typically on results of acute toxicity studies such as the determination of LD 50 values in animal experiments, notably rodents, via oral, inhaled, or external application. The experimental design measures the acute death rate of an agent. The toxicity class generally does not address issues of other potential harm of the agent, such as bioaccumulation, issues of ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#4_3210661262","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#5_3210664013","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_3182786751#1_3669823090","rank":3,"relevance":0}]}}
