{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"methyl bromide alternatives\" (+ nematodes nematicide fumigant) (+ ozone depletion phaseout \"montreal protocol\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_866237787#1_1763781074","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1506","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_866237787#1_1763781074\" , \"end_char\": 1751.000000 , \"headings\": \"Methyl Bromide Methyl Bromide What Is Methyl Bromide? Methyl bromide damages the ozone layer Find more information on methyl bromide: Key Resource The <cover>Phaseout of Methyl Bromide Methyl Bromide Alternatives Learn more: Methyl Bromide Critical Uses Methyl Bromide for Quarantine and Preshipment Quarantine Applications Preshipment Applications Apply for a Methyl Bromide Critical Use Exemption Pre-plant application form Post-harvest application form Methyl Bromide Reporting Forms \" , \"segment\": \"Methyl bromide is an odorless, colorless gas used to control a wide variety of pests in agriculture and shipping, including fungi, weeds, insects, nematodes (</cover>or roundworms), and rodents. Agricultural growers inject methyl bromide about two feet into the ground to sterilize the soil before crops are planted. Although the soil is covered with plastic tarps immediately after a treatment, 50 to 95 percent of the methyl bromide eventually enters the atmosphere. Learn more about soil fumigants. Methyl bromide is also used to treat commodities such as grapes, asparagus, logs, and other imported goods to prevent introducing "}}
