{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ bionematicide bionematicides bio-nematicides) \"plant-parasitic nematodes\" (+ chemical synthetic conventional) (+ environmental health toxicity))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1797328243#10_3591147408","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1530","rank":1,"snippet":"\"end_char\": 4168.000000 , \"headings\": \"Nematode Management in the Vegetable Garden 1 Nematode Management in the Vegetable Garden 1 What are nematodes? How do nematodes damage plants? Root-Knot Nematodes Ectoparasites Other Nematodes How do I know if nematodes are a problem? Handling Submitting a Sample What can I do about nematodes?<cover>Chemical Nematicides Bionematicides Resistance and Tolerance Soil Solarization Cover Crops Organic Amendments Planting Soil Tillage and Root Destruction Container Growing Summary Tables \" , \"segment\": \"Panel D, Theresa Friday UF/IFAS Extension How do nematodes damage plants? As plant-parasitic nematodes feed, they damage the root system and reduce the ability of a plant to obtain water and nutrients from the soil. When nematode numbers get high, and/or when environmental </cover>stresses occur, aboveground symptoms may become evident. Aboveground nematode symptoms often resemble nutrient deficiencies or drought stress. Symptoms include yellowing, wilting, and stunting (Figure 7, 8, 9). Figure 7. Nematode-damaged corn is stunted, yellow, and wilting. Damage occurs in patches. Credit: D. W. Dickson, UF/IFAS Figure 8.\" , \"start_char\": 3552.000000 , \"title"}}
