{"latest_judgment":{"docno":null,"relevance":0,"reason":"The aldicarb/Temik food-safety query returned no new unskipped results; it only retrieved the previously judged article about EPA/Bayer phaseout due to food-safety and child-health risks.","learned":"Additional aldicarb/Temik searches are now repeating the same strong phaseout evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"plant-parasitic nematodes\" \"plant parasitic nematodes\") (+ \"chemical nematicides\" nematicides) (+ \"human health\" environmental toxicity risks))","reason":"Broaden to review-style plant-parasitic-nematode literature without forcing ban/withdrawal terms, to find passages explaining that chemical nematicides declined or are avoided because of human-health, environmental, toxicity, and risk concerns."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"plant-parasitic nematodes\" \"plant parasitic nematodes\") (+ \"chemical nematicides\" nematicides) (+ \"human health\" environmental toxicity risks))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1179094178#12_2116808331","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1536","rank":1,"snippet":"2116808331\" , \"end_char\": 8415.000000 , \"headings\": \" \" , \"segment\": \"Symptoms include yellowing, wilting, browning, thinning out, or death. Often weeds such as spurge and sedges become prominent as the grass thins out . Nematode damage usually occurs in irregularly shaped patches that may enlarge slowly over time. Be aware that similar conditions may be caused by other factors such as localized soil conditions, fungi, or insects. Because <cover>plant-parasitic nematodes live in soil or roots, the pesticides used to kill them tend to be very toxic and are water soluble. Because of health risks and environmental concerns there are currently no toxic nematicides </cover>that are labeled for use on established home lawns. There are a number of products available for use on home lawns that are marketed as \"organic\", \"biological\" or \"non-toxic\" that claim to be suppressive to plant parasitic nematodes. Be aware that in order to be labeled for home use these types of products need to be safe, but do not need to be proven effective. Nernatologists at numerous universities have tested many of these products for efficacy, generally with disappointing results. "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"34/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1179094178#12_2116808331","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly answers the information need in a nematode-management context: nematicides used against soil/root plant-parasitic nematodes tend to be very toxic and water soluble, and because of health risks and environmental concerns there are currently no toxic nematicides labeled for established home lawns.","learned":"Nematicides have been lost or not labeled in some uses because their soil/root activity is associated with high toxicity, water solubility, health risks, and environmental concerns."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536","reason":"This broadened plant-parasitic-nematode query produced a new highly relevant result with direct health/environmental/toxicity evidence; continue to find more similar non-duplicate management documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1179094178#13_2116809753","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1536","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1179094178#13_2116809753\" , \"end_char\": 9056.000000 , \"headings\": \" \" , \"segment\": \"Because of health risks and <cover>environmental concerns there are currently no toxic nematicides that are labeled for use on established home lawns. There are a number of products available for use on home lawns that are marketed as \"organic\", \"biological\" or \"non-toxic\" that claim to be suppressive to plant parasitic nematodes. </cover>Be aware that in order to be labeled for home use these types of products need to be safe, but do not need to be proven effective. Nernatologists at numerous universities have tested many of these products for efficacy, generally with disappointing results. Adding soil amendments can help the grass tolerate nematode damage or possibly suppress nematode population densities. Remember that anything that can he done to improve root health is good. Colloidal phosphate incorporated into fine sand has been shown to help bermudagrass withstand attack by certain nematodes. Organic materials such as composted municipal sludge or manures can also promote grass health, and at the same time may stimulate "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"35/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1179094178#13_2116809753","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly supports the information need by stating that, because of health risks and environmental concerns, there are currently no toxic nematicides labeled for established home lawns; it also contrasts safer organic/biological products, showing safety-driven loss of conventional nematicide options.","learned":"Health risks and environmental concerns can eliminate labeled uses of toxic nematicides in certain settings, leaving only safer but often less-proven organic/biological products."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536","reason":"The current query is yielding new direct evidence on toxicity, health risks, environmental concerns, and lost/absent nematicide labels; continue to find additional non-duplicate documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_123562181#15_253441264","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1536","rank":4,"snippet":"parasitic nematodes. However, neuropeptides, unlike the small molecule ‘classical’ transmitters, (e.g., acetylcholine), have diverged considerably in structure during evolution and this presents the promise that drugs targeting peptidergic signalling in nematodes may have low mammalian <cover>toxicity. There has thus been considerable interest in the prospect of developing peptidomimetics as a novel approach for nematode control. A comparative analysis of the neuropeptide content for a range of nematodes has been comprehensively reviewed ( Marks and Maule, 2010; McVeigh et al., 2005) and may become an important consideration if this strategy is to be pursued. Progress has been made towards pairing the neuropeptide ligands with their cognate receptors in C. elegans ( Frooninckx et al., 2012) and this may point to new molecular targets for nematode control. A comparative analysis of the physiology and pharmacology of C. elegans with plant parasitic nematodes is more difficult as there is very little information available for these species in this regard.\" , \"start_char\": 8232.000000 , \"title\": \"Anthelmintic drugs and nematicides: </cover>studies in Caenorhabditis elegans - WormBook - NCBI Bookshelf\" , \"url\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK116072/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1797328243#10_3591147408","rank":3,"relevance":1}]}}
